  "dom_id": "step-q46835",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x}.$",
  "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=49$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x} & =\\frac{49-49}{7\\sqrt{49}-49}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{7\\sqrt{x}-x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{(7\\sqrt{x}-x)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{{(7\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{49x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(x-49)(7\\sqrt{x}+x)}{-x(x-49)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 49}\\frac{7\\sqrt{x}+x}{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{49}+49}{-49} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
